<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.hewenyu.herdr-server — the herdr server the bridge talks to.

  Install with deploy/install.sh. It substitutes every placeholder below (a name
  between double underscores) with an absolute path: launchd does no variable
  expansion at all, so "$HOME" and "~" inside a plist are literal characters,
  never a home directory.

  =====================================================================
  WHY THIS RUNS UNDER /usr/bin/env -i AND NOT JUST "herdr server"
  =====================================================================

  Measured (G7): herdr server hands its own environment, unchanged, to every
  pane it spawns. Start the server from inside a claude session and every claude
  in every pane inherits that session's CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION marker, and
  answers it by switching transcript saving OFF:

      ⚠ Transcript saving is off — inherited CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION marker

  Nothing crashes. Nothing is logged. There is simply no ~/.claude/projects
  transcript file afterwards, so the bridge's L2 mirror tails a file that will
  never exist and the phone shows an agent that never says anything. The feature
  is dead and the failure is silent — which is exactly why it is pinned here in
  the unit file rather than left to whoever types the start command.

  "env -i" therefore empties the environment and this job adds back only what a
  terminal multiplexer genuinely needs. Three notes on the shape of that fix:

    * It is an ALLOWLIST on purpose. CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION,
      CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT and every other CLAUDE_CODE_* variable, plus
      CLAUDECODE, CLAUDE_PID and CLAUDE_EFFORT, are all gone because nothing
      survives -i. A denylist could not express that anyway: `env -u` takes
      exact names, not the CLAUDE_CODE_* prefix, so it would need an edit every
      time a claude release invents another marker.

    * launchd's own EnvironmentVariables key cannot replace this. It MERGES with
      the per-user domain environment (anything set by `launchctl setenv`, or
      inherited when someone bootstraps this job by hand), it does not replace
      it. Only exec'ing env -i guarantees what the server does not have.

    * The allowlist below is HOME, PATH, SHELL, TERM, LANG and nothing else.
      Three of the absences are worth naming by hand, because every pane
      inherits this environment (G7) and the panes here run coding agents. Two
      of the three are variables launchd itself supplies to a gui job, so they
      are lost to the scrub rather than never having been there:

      XDG_CONFIG_HOME is gone, so herdr keeps its socket at
      ~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock — the same place the bridge looks by default.
      If you deliberately set XDG_CONFIG_HOME in your shell, add it to the
      allowlist below, or your terminal's `herdr` CLI and this server will use
      two different config directories and two different sockets.

      SSH_AUTH_SOCK is gone, so no pane has an ssh-agent. `git push` / `git
      fetch` over SSH from inside an agent then stops on a passphrase prompt or
      fails outright, and on a phone that reads as the agent freezing mid task
      with nothing on screen to explain it. Add it back with a line in the array
      below, next to HOME:

          <string>SSH_AUTH_SOCK=/private/tmp/com.apple.launchd.XXXX/Listeners</string>

      using the value `launchctl getenv SSH_AUTH_SOCK` prints. install.sh does
      not render this for you on purpose: that path carries a random component
      that launchd regenerates at every login, so a value baked in at install
      time is wrong after the next logout — and a stale SSH_AUTH_SOCK fails
      exactly the way a missing one does. If you want it to survive logins,
      resolve it when the job starts instead of when it is installed, by making
      the program `/bin/sh -c` and having the shell substitute it:

          /bin/sh -c 'exec /usr/bin/env -i HOME=... PATH=...
              SSH_AUTH_SOCK="$(/bin/launchctl getenv SSH_AUTH_SOCK)"
              /path/to/herdr server'

      i.e. three strings in the array: /bin/sh, -c, and that whole command as
      one. env -i still empties everything; the shell only computes one of the
      arguments it is handed, at start rather than at install.

      TMPDIR is gone, so tools in a pane fall back to /tmp instead of the per
      user /var/folders/... directory. Usually harmless: it costs the per user
      isolation and the automatic cleanup, and a few tools are noisier about it.

  Verify after install:  myrix doctor
  (the "herdr server environment is clean" check reads this process's real
  environment with `ps eww`, so it confirms the scrub actually happened.)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.hewenyu.herdr-server</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
    <string>-i</string>

    <!-- HOME: herdr resolves its config, socket and state from it. -->
    <string>HOME=__HOME__</string>

    <!-- PATH: this is the PATH every pane starts with, so it must contain the
         agents you intend to run (claude, codex) and their toolchains. It is
         NOT launchd's minimal default; install.sh builds it from the
         directories where it found herdr, claude and codex. -->
    <string>PATH=__CLEAN_PATH__</string>

    <!-- SHELL: what herdr spawns in a new pane. Without it the panes fall back
         to /bin/sh, which reads none of your login files. -->
    <string>SHELL=__LOGIN_SHELL__</string>

    <!-- TERM/LANG: the agent TUIs draw box-drawing characters and colour. A
         missing UTF-8 locale turns Claude's dialog frame into mojibake, and
         herdr's blocked-detection matches literal strings on that screen
         (G11), so a broken locale degrades to a silent "idle". -->
    <string>TERM=xterm-256color</string>
    <string>LANG=en_US.UTF-8</string>

    <string>__HERDR_BIN__</string>
    <string>server</string>
  </array>

  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>__HOME__</string>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Always restart. The product assumes this Mac is up and reachable; there
       is no offline compensation anywhere in the bridge, so a server that
       stayed down would just make the phone show nothing.
       Consequence worth knowing: `herdr server stop` will be undone by launchd
       within ThrottleInterval. To stop it for real:
           launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.hewenyu.herdr-server -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- If a hand-started herdr server already owns the socket, this copy exits
       immediately. 30s keeps that argument readable in the log instead of a
       spin; the loop ends by itself once the other server goes away. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__STATE_DIR__/log/herdr-server.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__STATE_DIR__/log/herdr-server.err.log</string>

  <!-- Deliberately no EnvironmentVariables dict: see the comment above. -->
</dict>
</plist>
